==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪkɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪkə/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bicker.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪkə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeg-|id=break}}
From {{inh|en|enm|bikeren|t=to attack}}, from {{der|en|dum|bicken|t=to stab, thrust, attack}} {{suf|en||er|id2=verbal frequentative|pos2=frequentative suffix}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bikjaną}} (compare {{cog|ang|becca|t=pickax}}, {{cog|nl|bikken|t=to hack}}, {{cog|de|picken|t=to peck, pick at}}, {{cog|non|bikkja|t=to plunge into water}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeg-|t=to smash, break}}. Compare also {{cog|nds-de|bickern|t=to nibble, gnaw}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[quarrel]] in a [[tiresome]], insulting manner.
#: {{ux|en|They '''bickered''' about dinner every evening.}}
#* {{RQ:Barrow Works|sermonname=Of Industry in our particular Calling, as Scholars|passage=petty things about which men cark and '''bicker'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 November 16|author=Paul Bigland|title=From rural branches to high-speed arteries|journal=RAIL|issue=970|page=55|text=Travelling with their granny, who seems more interested in her crossword puzzle than them, they '''bicker''' and fight in a futile bid to get her attention. Oh, the joys of travelling during the school holidays!}}
# To [[brawl]] or move [[tremulously]], quiver, shimmer (of a water stream, light, flame, etc.)
#* {{RQ:Thomson Castle of Indolence|stanza=III|page=2|passage=Mean time unnumber'd glittering Streamlets play'd, / And hurled every-where their Waters ſheen; / That, as they '''bicker'd''' through the ſunny Glade, / Though reſtleſs ſtill themſelves, a lulling Murmur made.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1886|title=[[s:The Brook (Tennyson)|The Brook]]|author=[[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Tennyson]]
|passage=I come from haunts of coot and hern, / I make a sudden sally, / And sparkle out among the fern, / To '''bicker''' down a valley.}}
# (of rain) To [[patter]].
# To [[skirmish]]; to exchange blows; to fight.
#* {{RQ:Suetonius Holland Caesars|section=5|page=243|passage=And at the field fought before ''Bebriacum'', ere the battailes joyned, tvvo [[eagle|Ægles]] had a conflict and '''bickered''' together in all their fights: and vvhen the one of them was foyled and overcome, a third came at the very inſtant from the ſunne riſing and chaſed the Victreſſe avvay.}}

